<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Kragujevac> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Kragujevac** ([Serbian Cyrillic](:Serbian_Cyrillic_alphabet): Крагујевац, Template:IPA-sh is the [fourth largest city](:List_of_cities_in_Serbia) in [Serbia](:Serbia) and the administrative centre of the [Šumadija District](:%C5%A0umadija_District). It is the historical centre of the geographical region of [Šumadija](:%C5%A0umadija) in central Serbia, and is situated on the banks of the [Lepenica River](:Lepenica_%28Great_Morava%29). According to the 2011 census[[update]](//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Kragujevac&action=edit), the city proper has a population of 150,835, while its administrative area comprises a total of 179,417 inhabitants.Kragujevac was the first capital of modern Serbia and the first constitution in the [Balkans](:Balkans), the [Sretenje Constitution](:Sretenje_Constitution), was proclaimed in the city in 1838. A unit of the [Scottish Women's Hospitals for Foreign Service](:Scottish_Women%27s_Hospitals_for_Foreign_Service) was located there in World War I. During the Second World War, Kragujevac was the site of a [massacre by the Nazis](:Kragujevac_massacre) in which 2,778 Serb men and boys were killed.Modern Kragujevac is known for its large munitions ([Zastava Arms](:Zastava_Arms%29) and automobile ([FCA Srbija](:FCA_Srbija%29) industries, as well as its status as an education centre housing the [University of Kragujevac](:University_of_Kragujevac), one of the region's largest higher education institutions."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
