<http://dbpedia.org/resource/GNR_521_Class> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The [Great Northern Railway](:Great_Northern_Railway_%28Great_Britain%29) **521 Class** was a class of [0-6-0](:0-6-0) [steam locomotives](:Steam_locomotive), introduced in 1911. They were designed by [Henry Ivatt](:Henry_Ivatt) for goods traffic. From 1912 to 1922 further examples, slightly modified by [Nigel Gresley](:Nigel_Gresley), were built and designated **536 Class**. The most obvious difference was in the front [sandboxes](:Sandbox_%28railways%29). These were below the [running plate](:Running_plate) on the 521 but above it, and merged with the front [splashers](:Splashers), on the 536. The boiler and firebox were also moved back, thus resulting in a shortened cab. The [London and North Eastern Railway](:London_and_North_Eastern_Railway) classified them both as **J6**.Initially, there was to be 120 members of this class. However, an order of ten was canceled so that Doncaster Works could build ten of Gresley's [N2](:GNR_Class_N2) Class [0-6-2](:0-6-2) Tanks, which shared the same cylinders, boilers, valve gear, and piston valves as the J6s. The J6s had [superheaters](:Superheater) and [piston valves](:Piston_valve_%28steam_engine%29) operated by [Stephenson valve gear](:Stephenson_valve_gear). The class earned the nickname \"Knick-Knacks\" due to the sound made by the locomotives when steam was shut off."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
