<http://dbpedia.org/resource/MV_Explorer_(1969)> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**MS *Explorer*** was a Liberian-registered [cruise ship](:Cruise_ship), the first vessel of that kind used specifically to sail the icy waters of the [Antarctic Ocean](:Antarctic_Ocean). She was the first cruise ship to sink there, after striking an [iceberg](:Iceberg) on 23 November 2007. All passengers and crew were rescued.The ship was commissioned and operated by the [Swedish](:Sweden) explorer [Lars-Eric Lindblad](:Lars-Eric_Lindblad). Its 1969 expeditionary cruise to [Antarctica](:Antarctica) was the forerunner for today's sea-based [tourism](:Tourism_in_Antarctica) in that region. The vessel was originally named **MS *Lindblad Explorer*** (until 1985), and **MS *Society Explorer*** (until 1992). Ownership of the vessel changed several times, the last owner being the [Toronto](:Toronto)-based travel company [G.A.P Adventures](:G%2EA%2EP_Adventures) which acquired *Explorer* in 2004.*Explorer* was abandoned in the early hours of 23 November 2007 after taking on water near the [South Shetland Islands](:South_Shetland_Islands) in the [Southern Ocean](:Southern_Ocean), an area which is usually stormy but was calm at the time. *Explorer* was confirmed by the [Chilean Navy](:Chilean_Navy) to have sunk at an approximate position of [62°24′S 57°16′W﻿ / ﻿62.400°S 57.267°W﻿ / -62.400; -57.267](https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=MV_Explorer_(1969)&params=62_24_S_57_16_W_), between the South Shetlands and [Grahams Land](:Antarctic_Peninsula) in the [Bransfield Strait](:Bransfield_Strait); where the depth is roughly 600 m. The [Royal Navy](:Royal_Navy) Antarctic Patrol Ship [*Endurance*](:HMS_Endurance_%28A171%29), whilst carrying out a hydrographic survey for the [British Antarctic Survey](:British_Antarctic_Survey) and at the request of the [Foreign and Commonwealth Office](:Foreign_and_Commonwealth_Office), later pinpointed *Explorer*'s final resting place as [62°24′17.57″S 57°11′46.49″W﻿ / ﻿62.4048806°S 57.1962472°W﻿ / -62.4048806; -57.1962472](https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=MV_Explorer_(1969)&params=62_24_17.57_S_57_11_46.49_W_), at an approximate depth of 1,130 m – a distance of 4,373 m from her reported sinking position. This is broadly consistent with the direction of the prevailing current."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
