<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Straight-four_engine> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A straight-four engine (also called an inline-four) is a four-cylinder piston engine where cylinders are arranged in a line along a common crankshaft. The vast majority of automotive four-cylinder engines use a straight-four layout (with the exceptions of the flat-four engines produced by Subaru and Porsche) and the layout is also very common in motorcycles and other machinery. Therefore the term \"four-cylinder engine\" is usually synonymous with straight-four engines. Most examples use liquid coolant to manage heat accumulation while air-cooled examples are also manufactured."@en .
