<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Córdoba,_Argentina> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Córdoba** (Template:IPA-es is a city in central [Argentina](:Argentina), in the foothills of the [Sierras Chicas](:Punilla_Valley) on the [Suquía River](:Primero_River), about 700 km (435 mi) northwest of [Buenos Aires](:Buenos_Aires). It is the capital of [Córdoba Province](:C%C3%B3rdoba_Province%2C_Argentina) and the [second most populous](:List_of_cities_in_Argentina_by_population) city in Argentina after Buenos Aires, with about 1.3 million inhabitants according to the 2010 census. It was founded on 6 July 1573 by [Jerónimo Luis de Cabrera](:Jer%C3%B3nimo_Luis_de_Cabrera), who named it after [Córdoba, Spain](:C%C3%B3rdoba%2C_Spain). It was one of the early Spanish colonial capitals of the region that is now Argentina (the oldest city is [Santiago del Estero](:Santiago_del_Estero), founded in 1553). The [National University of Córdoba](:National_University_of_C%C3%B3rdoba) is the oldest university of the country and the seventh to be inaugurated in [Spanish America](:Spanish_America). It was founded in 1613 by the [Jesuit Order](:Society_of_Jesus). Because of this, Córdoba earned the nickname *La Docta* (\"the learned\").Córdoba has many historical monuments preserved from [Spanish colonial rule](:Spanish_colonization_of_the_Americas), especially buildings of the [Catholic Church](:Catholic_Church). The most recognizable is perhaps the [Jesuit Block](:Jesuit_Block) (Spanish: *Manzana Jesuítica*), declared in 2000 as a [World Heritage Site](:World_Heritage_Site) by UNESCO which consists of a group of buildings dating from the 17th century, including the [Colegio Nacional de Monserrat](:Colegio_Nacional_de_Monserrat) and the colonial university campus. The campus belongs today to the historical museum of the [National University of Córdoba](:National_University_of_C%C3%B3rdoba), which has been the second-largest university in the country since the early 20th century (after the [University of Buenos Aires](:University_of_Buenos_Aires%29), in number of students, faculty, and academic programs. Córdoba is also known for its historical movements, such as [Cordobazo](:Cordobazo) and La Reforma del '18 (known as [University Revolution](:University_Revolution) in English)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
