<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Alfa_Romeo_6C> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **Alfa Romeo 6C** name was used on road, race, and sports cars produced between 1927 and 1954 by [Alfa Romeo](:Alfa_Romeo); the \"6C\" name refers to the six cylinders of the car's [straight-six engine](:Straight-six_engine). Bodies for these cars were made by [coachbuilders](:Coachbuilder) such as [James Young](:James_Young_%28coachbuilder%29), [Zagato](:Zagato), [Touring Superleggera](:Carrozzeria_Touring), [Castagna](:Carrozzeria_Castagna), and [Pinin Farina](:Pinin_Farina). Beginning in 1933 there was also a 6C version with an Alfa factory body, built in [Portello](:Portello%2C_Milan). In the early 1920s [Vittorio Jano](:Vittorio_Jano) received a commission to create a lightweight, high performance vehicle to replace the [Giuseppe Merosi](:Giuseppe_Merosi) designed [RL](:Alfa_Romeo_RL) and [RM](:Alfa_Romeo_RM) models. The car was introduced in April 1925 at the Salone dell' Automobile di Milano as the 6C 1500. It was based on Alfa's [P2](:Alfa_Romeo_P2) Grand Prix car, using a single overhead cam 1,487 cc in-line six-cylinder engine, producing 44 horsepower. In 1928 the 1500 Sport was presented, which was the first Alfa Romeo road car with double overhead camshafts."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
